     ],
     "note": "Bug in tokamak, not bs; reported here in upstream style, not patched. Title: meta.transmute reads through a misaligned pointer (dom.document.test_0 panics with 'incorrect alignment'). Where: src/meta.zig:221 `pub inline fn transmute(comptime T: type, val: anytype) T { const tmp: *const T = @ptrCast(@alignCast(&val)); return tmp.*; }`, called by src/dom/local_name.zig:14 `meta.transmute(u128, x)` with x a ShortString, an extern struct { len21: u8, buf: [15]u8 } of alignment 1. `&val` is only 1-aligned, so the @alignCast to *const u128 (alignment 16) is illegal behavior whenever the parameter's stack slot is not 16-aligned; the safety check panics. Upstream 0.17.0 Debug passes tokamak's test only because its frame happens to put that slot on a 16-byte boundary. Reproduction (panics under upstream 0.17.0 and bs alike): const ShortString = extern struct { len21: u8 = 1, buf: [15]u8 = @splat(0) }; inline fn transmute(comptime T: type, val: anytype) T { const tmp: *const T = @ptrCast(@alignCast(&val)); return tmp.*; } noinline fn parse(n: u8) u128 { var pad: [3]u8 = .{ n, n, n }; _ = &pad; return transmute(u128, ShortString{ .len21 = pad[1] }); } pub fn main() void { _ = parse(4); } Suggested fix: `return @bitCast(val)` where the sizes match, or copy the bytes: `var tmp: T = undefined; @memcpy(std.mem.asBytes(&tmp), std.mem.asBytes(&val)); return tmp;`. With the copy, bs and upstream give the same 88 passed, 1 failed (http.client: AddressInUse in both)."
    }
